<h4 id="2.3-Encoding-Categorical-Variables-(Mean-Encoding)"><strong>2.3 Encoding Categorical Variables (Mean Encoding)</strong><a class="anchor-link" href="#2.3-Encoding-Categorical-Variables-(Mean-Encoding)">¶</a></h4><p>Since categorical variables are non-numeric, we use Mean Encoding, where each category is replaced by the mean target variable (default probability) within that category.</p>
<p><strong>Why Use Mean Encoding?</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Captures Relationship with Target –</strong> Unlike one-hot encoding, mean encoding captures how each category affects the target variable.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Reduces Dimensionality –</strong> Unlike one-hot encoding, it avoids creating multiple binary columns, making the model more efficient.</p>
</li>
</ul>
