<h4 id="3.1-Why-Linear-Regression-is-Unsuitable-for-Classification"><strong>3.1 Why Linear Regression is Unsuitable for Classification</strong><a class="anchor-link" href="#3.1-Why-Linear-Regression-is-Unsuitable-for-Classification">¶</a></h4><p>Linear regression is designed for continuous outcomes, whereas classification problems deal with categorical labels. If we apply linear regression to a binary classification problem, the predicted values can fall outside the range [0,1], which is not interpretable as a probability.</p>
<p><strong>Mathematical Explanation</strong>
In a linear regression model, the prediction function is:</p>
<pre><code>                    𝑦^ = 𝛽0 + 𝛽1𝑋

</code></pre>
<p>However, for a classification problem where 𝑦 takes values {0,1}, this formulation is problematic because:</p>
<ul>
<li>The output 𝑦^ can be any real number, but probabilities must be between 0 and 1.</li>
<li>Linear regression does not model the probability of class membership well, especially when data is non-linearly separable.</li>
</ul>
<p>Thus, we need a transformation that ensures our predictions always remain within the range (0,1).</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
