<h4 id="3.2-Logistic-(Sigmoid)-Function-for-Binary-Classification"><strong>3.2 Logistic (Sigmoid) Function for Binary Classification</strong><a class="anchor-link" href="#3.2-Logistic-(Sigmoid)-Function-for-Binary-Classification">¶</a></h4><p>To constrain predictions to [0,1], we use the sigmoid function, also known as the logistic function, which maps any real number to a probability between 0 and 1:</p>
<pre><code>                            𝜎(𝑧) = 1/1+(𝑒)^−𝑧



</code></pre>
<p>where z is the linear combination of input features:</p>
<pre><code>                         𝑧 = 𝛽0+𝛽1𝑋1+𝛽2𝑋2+...+𝛽𝑛𝑋𝑛

</code></pre>
<p>Key Properties of the Sigmoid Function:</p>
<ul>
<li>If z is large (positive), σ(z)≈1, meaning a high probability of class 1.</li>
<li>If z is small (negative), σ(z)≈0, meaning a high probability of class 0.</li>
<li>If z=0, then σ(0)=0.5, meaning equal probability for both classes.</li>
</ul>
