<p>Our evaluation of Logistic Regression and Decision Tree Classifier for loan default prediction highlighted key trade-offs between accuracy, recall, and interpretability. Logistic Regression achieved a high accuracy of 92%, but its recall for defaulters was only 1%, making it ineffective in identifying high-risk borrowers. In contrast, the Decision Tree had a lower accuracy of 85%, yet it performed better in detecting defaults (17% recall for class 1). Both models struggled with class imbalance, requiring SMOTE or adjusted class weights for improvement. Feature analysis revealed that loan amount, interest rate, loan-to-income ratio, employment length, and credit history are strong predictors of default. To enhance risk assessment, financial institutions should implement class imbalance handling, incorporate debt-to-income ratio and credit bureau data, and move towards probability-based loan approvals rather than strict binary classifications. Regular model retraining and real-time monitoring are crucial for adapting to changing borrower behaviors. For optimal results, a hybrid approach combining Logistic Regression for interpretability and Decision Trees (or XGBoost) for recall improvement is recommended. By leveraging advanced machine learning techniques and domain knowledge, lenders can make data-driven, risk-aware lending decisions, minimizing financial losses while maximizing loan approval efficiency.</p>
